= Selection bias — Technical =

== Selection mechanism ==
Let S indicate inclusion in the analyzed sample.

Inference from:

<pre>
P(Y | X, S=1)
</pre>

to a target population can fail when S depends on variables related to X, Y, or their causes.

== Collider selection ==
If:

<pre>
X → S ← Y
</pre>

conditioning on S can induce dependence between X and Y even when they are otherwise independent.

More complex causal graphs produce analogous selection pathways.

== Forms ==
Selection mechanisms include sampling-frame bias, volunteer/self-selection, nonresponse, informative censoring, attrition, case-control selection, and conditioning on survival.

== Transportability ==
Correcting selection requires assumptions about how sampled and target populations differ.

Weighting or standardization can adjust observed covariates but cannot automatically fix unmeasured selection.

== References ==
* Hernán & Robins, ''Causal Inference: What If''.
* epidemiologic selection-bias literature.
